  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\n  We address the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem by refining and scaling the initial Thue-Morse-inspired solution. The core idea is to use multi-resolution optimization, where we first optimize the function on a coarse grid (64 points) and then iteratively refine the solution by doubling the resolution (128, 256, 512 points) while maintaining the parent solution. This strategy leverages the scalability of correlated functions across grid sizes, allowing us to escape local optima found in single-resolution searches.\n\n  The algorithm uses a combination of random restarts and simulated annealing at each grid level, carefully managing time constraints. Critical improvements include:\n  1. A hybrid optimization strategy combining random restarts with temperature-scheduled simulated annealing\n  2. Progressive grid scaling (from 64 to 512 points via interpolation)\n  3. Explicit geometric constraints in candidate generation\n  4. Time-dependent temperature adjustment ensuring thermal equilibrium before termination\n\n  Mathematical specification:\n  - Grid representation: Piecewise constant function h(x) on [0,2] with n_points elements\n  - Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i, \u2211h[i] = n_points/2 (ensuring integral 1)\n  - Objective: C5 = max_{k} (optical correlation integral) = max_k \u222bh(t)(1-h(t+k))dt\n  - Evaluation method: Discrete correlation using np.correlate() then scaling by dx=2/n_points\n  - Optimization strategy: Hybrid annealing with restarts and grid interpolation\n\n  Implementation procedure:\n  1. Initialization:\n      - Use provided initial_h_values if available\n      - Set time budgets and define grid scaling factors\n      - Compute initial score and parent vectors for transfer\n\n  2. Grid doubling workflow:\n      - Starting from current parent h_p with N samples\n      - Double N to N'=2*N samples\n      - Interpolate using np.interp() preserving integral\n      - Apply local perturbations and repair feasibility\n      - Run annealed optimization with time-dependent steps\n\n  3. Annealing parameters:\n      - Temperature decay: T = T0 * (1 - t_total/t_max)**\u03b2\n      - Step size adapter: \u03b4 = \u03b4_initial * (T_range/T)**\u03b3\n      - Geometric constraints: Candidate generation biased toward current parent\n\n  4. Candidate generation:\n      - Maintain geometric coherence with parent by:\n          a) Local perturbations: Random exchanges of mass between segments\n          b) Geometric flips: Swap values while preserving spatial proximity\n      - Repair constraint violations: clip values to [0,1] and adjust sum\n\n  5. Score computation:\n      - For candidate h, compute C5 = max(np.correlate(h,1-h,'full')) * (2/n_points)\n      - Compare against current best solution across grids\n\n  6. Termination conditions:\n      - Timed out: Return best solution found so far\n      - Temperature too low: Switch to local refinement\n      - Grid limit reached (512 points): No further scaling\n\n  Search choices and useful variations:\n  - Tunable parameters: \u03b4_range=[0.1,2.0], \u03b2=0.7, \u03b3=0.5\n  - Grid sequence: [64, 128, 256, 512] (powers of two for interpolation)\n  - Time allocation: Fractional budgets [0.15,0.25,0.3,0.3] for each grid doubling\n  - Neighborhood exploration: Local transfers at scale \u03b4, global resets at higher \u03b4\n  - Restart policy: Random baseline only at grid boundaries, local within-grid restarts\n\n  Numerical robustness:\n  - Memory: 3 * pop_size * n_points samples (50*512=25600 floats \u2248 200 MB)\n  - Time: Each annealing step processes one candidate, correlation (O(n_points) via FFT)\n  - Repair: Uses explicit projection handle: clip then scale sum\n  - Fallback: Retains previous best solution\n\n  Runtimes safeguards:\n  - Wall-clock time tracking with time_left() computation\n  - Generation-based termination: Switch to local search when cooling complete\n  - Time grid: Precomputed allocations based on sequence fractions\n  - Failsafe: Returning earliest optimal solution\n\n  Return contract:\n  - Format (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n  - Time-completion: Presently optimizes across available time\n  - Projection handled via direct interpolation and clipping",
